.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 5.19, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.45, "baseline_margin": 6.02, "analyst_adjustment": -0.02, "key_factors": ["Sand leads all twenty fall polls, averaging roughly +7 with the latest surveys still showing +4 or better", "Open seat removes the Republican incumbency advantage in a state that leans R by about six points", "Record $81M fundraising gives Sand a cash edge and an unusually large donor base", "Eight raters call it a Democratic lean or tilt and markets price Sand near 87%"], "rationale": "Every poll this fall shows Sand ahead by four to twelve points despite Iowa's Republican lean, and the open seat, his record fundraising edge, and residency questions around Lahn outweigh the state's fundamentals. Ratings and prediction markets converge on a clear but not safe Democratic advantage.", "watch": "A Trump-driven late turnout surge or full consolidation of Republican voters behind Lahn could compress the margin in the final days.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open as incumbent Kim Reynolds retires, pitting Democrat Rob Sand against Republican Zach Lahn in a state that has leaned Republican by about six points in recent cycles. Every poll taken this fall, twenty in all, shows Sand ahead, with margins ranging from four to twelve points and an average near seven. The most recent surveys, including a Republican-sponsored poll in mid-October, still put him up at least four.\n\nSand has shattered state fundraising records with more than $81 million raised and a large donor base, while questions about whether Lahn resides in Iowa have trailed his campaign. Ratings across eight outlets call the race a Democratic lean or tilt, and prediction markets price Sand's chances in the high eighties. A late Republican turnout push led by Trump remains the main threat to that picture.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin":